    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.bipartite_maximum_matching - EdgeCase\",\n    \"isPartOf\": {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.bipartite_maximum_matching.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.bipartite_maximum_matching()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function\n    .doc-label .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: >-\n    Find a maximum matching of a bipartite graph (Hopcroft-Karp algorithm).\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.bipartite_maximum_matching()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function\n    .doc-label .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: >-\n    Find a maximum matching of a bipartite graph (Hopcroft-Karp algorithm).\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.bipartite_maximum_matching()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: >-\n  Find a maximum matching of a bipartite graph (Hopcroft-Karp algorithm).\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nFind a maximum matching of a bipartite graph (Hopcroft-Karp algorithm).\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L83477-L83569){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.bipartite_maximum_matching(top_nodes=None)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Bipartite maximum matching](../assets/graphs/bipartite-maximum-matching.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"Three applicants, Ann, Bob, and Cy, in a column on the left and three jobs, Dev, Ops, and QA, on the right. Ann and Bob can only do Dev, and Cy can do Ops or QA, so at most two pairs form: Ann–Dev and Cy–Ops, in the accent color with their endpoints. Bob and QA stay unmatched. The card lists the two pairs and the size 2.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nIn a bipartite graph the nodes split into two sides and every edge joins the two sides,\nas in workers and jobs, students and schools, or reviewers and papers. A maximum\nmatching pairs as many nodes across the sides as possible, with each node in at most\none pair. This method finds one with the Hopcroft-Karp algorithm, which is much faster\nthan the general `maximum_weight_matching()` on large bipartite graphs.\n\nThe sides are found by 2-coloring the graph (ignoring edge direction) unless they are\ngiven with `top_nodes=`. The size of a maximum matching does not depend on which valid\nsplit is used. Self-loops are not allowed (they make a graph non-bipartite).\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[top_nodes]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [list | None]{.doc-parameter-annotation} [=]{.doc-parameter-default-sep .op} [None]{.doc-parameter-default}</code>\n\n:   Optional list of the nodes on one side (IDs or labels); all other nodes form the\n    other side. If `None` (default), the sides are found automatically.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [list[int]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   The IDs of the matched edges, in ascending order.\n:::\n\n## Raises {.doc-raises}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [ValueError]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   If the graph is not bipartite, or `top_nodes=` leaves an edge within one side.\n:::\n\n## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}\n\nHopcroft and Karp (1973) augment along many shortest augmenting paths at once: each\nphase runs a breadth-first search from all free top nodes to layer the graph, then a\ndepth-first search finds a maximal set of vertex-disjoint shortest augmenting paths in\nthat layering. Only $O(\\sqrt{V})$ phases are needed, so the running time is\n$O(E \\sqrt{V})$. By König's theorem the matching size equals the size of a minimum\nvertex cover (see `bipartite_vertex_cover()`).\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~bipartite_minimum_weight_full_matching`) \n\n:   The cheapest matching that covers one side.\n\n[](`~bipartite_vertex_cover`) \n\n:   The smallest cover, as large as the matching.\n\n[](`~maximum_weight_matching`) \n\n:   The same for graphs that aren't bipartite.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nThree applicants and three jobs. Ann and Bob can only do the first job between them,\nso at most two of the three jobs can be filled:\n\n::: {#c0dc45a7 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ng = ec.Graph.from_edges(\n    [(\"Ann\", \"Dev\"), (\"Bob\", \"Dev\"), (\"Cy\", \"Ops\"), (\"Cy\", \"QA\")]\n)\nmatching = g.bipartite_maximum_matching(top_nodes=[\"Ann\", \"Bob\", \"Cy\"])\n\nlabel = g.get_label_from_id\n[tuple(label(n) for n in g.get_node_ids_by_edge_id(e)) for e in matching]\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n[('Ann', 'Dev'), ('Cy', 'Ops')]\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### One task too many\n\n`teams()` links the 8 workers of a made-up software team to the 8 tasks of a release that\neach can do. Only Omar and Kofi can do any of Database, DevOps and Security, and two\npeople can't take three tasks, so at most seven are covered:\n\n::: {#0d0e0691 .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nteam = ec.teams()\nmatched = team.bipartite_maximum_matching()\n\nlabel = team.get_label_from_id\ncovered = {n for e in matched for n in team.get_node_ids_by_edge_id(e)}\nlen(matched), [label(n) for n in team.get_node_ids() if n not in covered]\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n(7, ['Luis', 'DevOps'])\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n::: {#89040cd3 .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nteam.layout_bipartite(align=\"horizontal\", aspect_ratio=0.4).plot(\n    layout=\"pos\", highlight_edges=matched, label_by=\"label\", height=300\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-63cc1385:#d4d4d8;--ect-879b7ebc:#949497;--ect-b9f8de6d:#872d08;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-63cc1385:#3f3f46;--ect-879b7ebc:#2c2c31;--ect-b9f8de6d:#ae500f;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}}:is(html.quarto-light, body.quarto-light, html[data-bs-theme=\"light\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"true\"], body.vscode-light, body.vscode-high-contrast-light, html[theme=\"light\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-63cc1385:#d4d4d8;--ect-879b7ebc:#949497;--ect-b9f8de6d:#872d08;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}:is(html.quarto-dark, body.quarto-dark, html[data-bs-theme=\"dark\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"false\"], body.vscode-dark, body.vscode-high-contrast:not(.vscode-high-contrast-light), html[theme=\"dark\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,